1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,702
In the summer of seventeen fifty-two, a
grown man walked out into a thunderstorm

2
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:08,900
and flew a kite into the clouds, on
purpose.

3
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:10,980
Why would anyone do that?

4
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:16,626
And why does almost every tall building
you have ever seen still carry the answer

5
00:00:16,666 --> 00:00:17,466
on its roof?

6
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:24,429
By the end of this film, you'll know how a
kite, a key and a glass jar proved what

7
00:00:24,469 --> 00:00:30,720
lightning really is, and how one simple
idea stopped it burning our towns down.

8
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,420
For thousands of years, lightning was a
mystery.

9
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:41,111
It fell without warning, split trees,
killed animals in the fields and set roofs

10
00:00:41,151 --> 00:00:41,951
alight.

11
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:45,960
Many people believed it was a punishment
sent from the heavens.

12
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,200
Picture yourself in a town in those years,
on a hot summer night.

13
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:57,076
You would see the sky flash white, hear
the thunder crack right overhead, and

14
00:00:57,116 --> 00:01:00,200
smell the smoke as a barn went up in
flames.

15
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,200
And you could do nothing but wait for the
next strike.

16
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,760
The tallest buildings were hit most of
all.

17
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,380
Church steeples were struck again and
again.

18
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:15,811
And when a storm came, the bell-ringers
were sent up the tower to ring the bells,

19
00:01:15,851 --> 00:01:20,000
because people believed the sound would
drive the lightning away.

20
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:22,820
It was the worst place to stand.

21
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:28,413
One eighteenth-century count found more
than a hundred bell-ringers killed by

22
00:01:28,453 --> 00:01:30,520
lightning in just a few decades.

23
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:32,600
So what was lightning?

24
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:34,060
A punishment?

25
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:35,380
A kind of fire?

26
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,780
Or something else, something you could
catch and study?

27
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:43,700
In the seventeen-forties, electricity was
a party trick.

28
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:49,025
Travelling lecturers rubbed glass tubes
with silk and made sparks fly from

29
00:01:49,065 --> 00:01:50,500
people's fingers and hair.

30
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,060
Audiences paid to feel the shock.

31
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:58,594
In Philadelphia, a printer called Benjamin
Franklin saw one of these shows, and could

32
00:01:58,634 --> 00:02:00,220
not stop thinking about it.

33
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:04,660
He was so curious that he handed his
printing business to a partner, so he

34
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:06,720
would have time to study electricity.

35
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:11,500
His most exciting tool was a new invention
called a Leyden jar.

36
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:17,878
It was a glass jar lined with metal foil,
and it could store electricity, then let

37
00:02:17,918 --> 00:02:21,700
it all go at once, in a bright spark with
a loud crack.

38
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:24,820
And that spark reminded Franklin of
something.

39
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:26,420
A flash of light.

40
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:27,940
A crack of noise.

41
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:30,060
A sharp smell in the air.

42
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:33,140
It was like a tiny thunderstorm in a jar.

43
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:38,000
So in November seventeen forty-nine, he
wrote a list in his notebook.

44
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,040
Twelve ways a spark was just like
lightning.

45
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:44,620
They both gave light of the same colour.

46
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,560
They both moved in crooked lines.

47
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:52,140
They both made a crack, set things on fire
and could kill animals.

48
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:55,100
But a list of likenesses isn't proof.

49
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:00,240
At the bottom of the page, Franklin wrote
five famous words.

50
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:03,640
Let the experiment be made.

51
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:07,840
But how do you catch a bolt of lightning,
without it killing you?

52
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:09,680
Franklin had a plan.

53
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:14,940
Points, he had found, were very good at
drawing electricity out of the air.

54
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:21,240
So he proposed putting a tall iron rod,
sharp at the tip, on top of a high tower.

55
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:26,775
If lightning really was electricity, the
rod should pull sparks out of a passing

56
00:03:26,815 --> 00:03:27,615
storm cloud.

57
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:31,120
He published the idea, and it crossed the
ocean.

58
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:38,967
In May seventeen fifty-two, in a garden at
Marly, near Paris, French scientists put

59
00:03:39,007 --> 00:03:41,920
up a tall pointed rod and tried it.

60
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:46,160
As a storm cloud passed, sparks jumped
from the rod.

61
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:47,620
It worked.

62
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,820
But Franklin didn't know yet.

63
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:52,660
News took weeks to cross the Atlantic.

64
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:56,060
And Philadelphia had no tower tall enough.

65
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,980
So, about a month later, he had another
idea.

66
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:03,800
A kite could fly higher than any steeple.

67
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:09,507
He made one from a silk handkerchief and
two crossed sticks of cedar, so it would

68
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:10,640
survive the rain.

69
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,220
On top he fixed a sharp wire.

70
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:20,495
The string was ordinary twine, and near
his hand he tied an iron key, then a

71
00:04:20,535 --> 00:04:22,640
ribbon of silk to hold it by.

72
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,120
The silk was the clever part.

73
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:30,120
Wet twine would carry electricity, but dry
silk would not.

74
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:36,180
So Franklin stood inside a shed, out of
the rain, and kept the ribbon dry.

75
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,000
He told almost no one.

76
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:41,940
He was afraid people would laugh if it
failed.

77
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,120
Only his son William, a young man of about
twenty-one, came along to help.

78
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:53,380
William ran across the field and the kite
climbed into the dark clouds.

79
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:55,740
Then they waited.

80
00:04:56,580 --> 00:05:02,390
Imagine standing in that doorway, the
storm overhead, holding a string that runs

81
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:03,600
up into the sky.

82
00:05:04,820 --> 00:05:07,820
What would you feel, waiting for something
to happen?

83
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:11,020
For a long time, nothing did.

84
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:14,940
Then Franklin noticed the loose threads of
the twine.

85
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:18,964
They were standing up on end, and pushing
away from each other.

86
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,360
Slowly, Franklin brought his knuckle close
to the key.

87
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,920
A spark jumped across, with a tiny snap.

88
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:31,760
Exactly the kind of spark he had made a
thousand times in his own room.

89
00:05:32,340 --> 00:05:38,024
Then he touched the key to his Leyden jar,
and filled it, with electricity from the

90
00:05:38,064 --> 00:05:38,340
sky.

91
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:43,100
It behaved in every way like the
electricity he made with glass and silk.

92
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:47,760
Lightning and the spark in the jar were
the same thing.

93
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:49,240
Electricity.

94
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,100
And here is the part that most people get
wrong.

95
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:55,240
The kite was never struck by lightning.

96
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:59,120
If it had been, Franklin would almost
certainly have died.

97
00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:05,520
Instead, the kite gathered a little of the
storm's electric charge, which ran quietly

98
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,580
down the wet string to the key.

99
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:09,400
It was still dangerous.

100
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:15,223
The next summer, a scientist in Russia
called Georg Richmann was trying a similar

101
00:06:15,263 --> 00:06:18,860
experiment with a rod during a storm, and
he was killed.

102
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:22,520
Franklin had been careful, and lucky.

103
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:28,120
In October, Franklin published how to do
the kite experiment in his own newspaper.

104
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:33,918
His letters were read at the Royal Society
in London, and the next year it gave him

105
00:06:33,958 --> 00:06:36,100
its highest award, the Copley Medal.

106
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,740
But Franklin wasn't only interested in
proving an idea.

107
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:41,780
He wanted to use it.

108
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:47,591
If a pointed rod could pull electricity
from the sky, could it also keep lightning

109
00:06:47,631 --> 00:06:48,924
away from people's homes?

110
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:55,600
In September seventeen fifty-two, Franklin
put an iron rod on top of his own house.

111
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,420
He ran a wire from it into the house and
hung two little bells by the stairs.

112
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:05,440
Whenever a storm cloud passed overhead,
the bells rang on their own.

113
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:08,620
Then he told everyone how to protect their
homes.

114
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:14,640
In his popular yearly almanac, Poor
Richard, he printed simple instructions.

115
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:20,885
Fix an iron rod up the outside wall, with
a sharp point high above the roof, and the

116
00:07:20,925 --> 00:07:23,280
other end buried deep in damp ground.

117
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:25,020
Here's how it works.

118
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:29,800
Lightning is a giant spark, hotter than
the surface of the Sun.

119
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:32,840
It's looking for the easiest path to the
ground.

120
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:38,921
A wooden roof or a stone steeple is a
terrible path, so it bursts into flame or

121
00:07:38,961 --> 00:07:39,840
cracks apart.

122
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:43,020
But a metal rod is an easy path.

123
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:47,702
So the lightning takes the rod, round the
outside of the building, and goes

124
00:07:47,742 --> 00:07:49,240
harmlessly into the earth.

125
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:52,060
Franklin never patented his lightning rod.

126
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:54,140
He wanted everyone to have it.

127
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:59,560
Within a few years, rods were going up on
churches, houses and ships' masts.

128
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:04,300
And one by one, church towers became safer
places to stand.

129
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:06,600
Why was this so groundbreaking?

130
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:12,436
Because it was one of the very first times
an idea from a laboratory reached out and

131
00:08:12,476 --> 00:08:16,420
protected ordinary people from one of
nature's deadliest forces.

132
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:22,189
It showed that the wild things in the sky
follow rules, and that once you understand

133
00:08:22,229 --> 00:08:24,000
the rules, you can stay safe.

134
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:28,920
So next time a storm rolls in, look up at
the tallest building you can see.

135
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:33,100
There's a good chance you'll spot a thin
metal rod at the very top.

136
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:38,571
Some tall towers are struck by lightning
more than twenty times in a single year,

137
00:08:38,611 --> 00:08:39,640
and stay standing.

138
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:42,980
A kite, a key and a jar.

139
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:47,922
Together, they took lightning out of the
realm of mystery, and put a little piece

140
00:08:47,962 --> 00:08:49,000
of it on every roof.

141
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:54,412
Long before Franklin, a young man in an
English garden asked a different question

142
00:08:54,452 --> 00:08:55,260
about the sky.

143
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:59,576
If an apple falls to the ground, why
doesn't the Moon?
